From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 11 Jul 2026 14:03:14 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/28] node-executor: publish source packages atomically

Lease source packages across execute and analyze, retain only zero-owner entries, and enforce count and byte limits for source and external packages. Reference-count external ownership, keep same-key materialization single-flight, and stage, validate, and atomically publish complete package trees.

Expose a preparation request that acquires and releases a package lease without importing application modules. This provides a strict boundary for materializing and verifying a package before an executor accepts calls that can evaluate application code.

Run dependency installation in a private directory through a bounded asynchronous process-group supervisor. Ordinary completion and install timeout wait for the supervisor to close. Abrupt executor-generation death triggers best-effort process-group termination, but Rust receives no descendant-exit acknowledgement.

Enforce extracted and compressed dependency size limits before archive publication or upload. Bound archive inspection and upload, reject non-success responses, sanitize failures, and remove retained package state during Lambda reset.

Reference-count stack roots, resolve frames directly from registered module paths, restore the process stack hook before every invocation, and expose bounded package and stack-format metrics. Cover concurrent ownership, failed publication, build timeout, upload, cleanup, preparation, and stack-hook regressions.

Correct the package verification guidance to use each package's defined lint script, targeted Prettier checks, and the existing Turbo build command.
